<!-- ============ COMPOSABLE POSITION ============ -->
<!-- This was two 53-word paragraphs carrying the whole positioning argument, which is
     the hardest thing on the page to skim. The argument is a three-way comparison, so
     it is laid out as one: the two failure modes, then the position between them. -->
<section class="section ac-purple sunk" id="composable">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Where We Stand on Composable</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Unified at the core. Composable at the edge.</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Both extremes have a real failure mode. The useful question is which parts should never be allowed to disagree with each other.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="arx3">
      <div class="arx3-col">
        <span class="arx3-cap">Monolithic suite</span>
        <p class="arx3-sum">Every module from one vendor.</p>
        <ul class="arx3-list arx3-bad">
          <li>Take the whole suite or none of it</li>
          <li>Wait years for any one module to change</li>
          <li>No room for a system you already run</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="arx3-col">
        <span class="arx3-cap">Fully composable</span>
        <p class="arx3-sum">Every domain a separate service.</p>
        <ul class="arx3-list arx3-bad">
          <li>Independently versioned services that agree on nothing</li>
          <li>A regression cycle across a dozen contracts per release</li>
          <li>Sync windows where two systems hold different answers</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="arx3-col arx3-col-ours">
        <span class="arx3-cap">Teamwork Commerce</span>
        <p class="arx3-sum">One core. Composable around it.</p>
        <ul class="arx3-list arx3-good">
          <li>One data model across POS, inventory, orders, product and customer, with no sync window</li>
          <li>One release and one upgrade path, not a regression cycle per connected app</li>
          <li>Open APIs, so the core is readable and writable by the rest of your stack</li>
          <li>Custom services run alongside the core, so bespoke work never becomes a version you are frozen on</li>
          <li>Interface composition per retailer: module sequence and exposure are configured, not coded</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="arx3-why"><strong>Why the core is the line:</strong> selling, inventory, orders, product, customer and identity are the domains where two systems disagreeing becomes a wrong answer in front of a customer. Everything outside that is yours to compose.</p>
    <p class="fine-note">If your target state mandates fully independent, separately deployable services for every retail domain, that is a genuine architectural difference. Worth testing in a technical review rather than discovering in an RFP response.</p>
  </div>
</section>

